var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#GDML","page":"GDML","text":"This example reads geometries from GDML files, the geometry exchange format of Geant4 and ROOT: test geometries of the shapes and the CMS detector (2018). It draws them and makes X-ray images of the muon barrel of CMS.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"GDML"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#Table-of-contents","page":"GDML","text":"Pages = [\"GDML.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#Loading-the-modules","page":"GDML","text":"using Geom4hep\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, LScene, Axis, heatmap!, DataAspect\n\nThe GDML files are in the examples directory of the package\n\ngdmldir = joinpath(pkgdir(Geom4hep), \"examples\");","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#Reading-a-GDML-file","page":"GDML","text":"read_gdml returns the world volume of the file, with the floating point type given as second argument (Float64 by default). Lengths are converted to mm and angles to radians.\n\nworld = read_gdml(joinpath(gdmldir, \"polyhedra.gdml\"))\n[(d.volume.label, d.volume.shape) for d in world.daughters]\n\n3-element Vector{Tuple{String, Geom4hep.Polyhedra{Float64, VS} where VS<:AbstractVector{Geom4hep.PolyhedraSection{Float64}}}}:\n (\"HRCF\", Polyhedra{Float64}(nsides = 18, nsections = 5, z = [-1158.0, -695.4000000000001, -649.7800000000002, 60.789999999999964, 91.27000000000044, 1141.0], ϕ₀ = 6.1086523819801535, Δϕ = 6.283185307179586))\n (\"STEP\", Polyhedra{Float64}(nsides = 8, nsections = 2, z = [-300.0, 0.0, 300.0], ϕ₀ = 0.0, Δϕ = 6.283185307179586))\n (\"OPEN\", Polyhedra{Float64}(nsides = 6, nsections = 2, z = [-200.0, 0.0, 200.0], ϕ₀ = 0.7853981633974483, Δϕ = 4.71238898038469))\n\nThe volumes can be drawn as any other hierarchy of volumes:\n\nfig = Figure(size=(1000, 400))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 1], show_axis=false), world)\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 2], show_axis=false), read_gdml(joinpath(gdmldir, \"torus.gdml\")))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 3], show_axis=false), read_gdml(joinpath(gdmldir, \"polycones.gdml\")))\nfig\n\n<img width=1000 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Reading a GDML file"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#The-CMS-detector","page":"GDML","text":"The GDML file of CMS (2018) has about 20 000 placements of 3 800 volumes. It is read in a few seconds. Here in single precision, as needed on GPUs.\n\ncms = read_gdml(joinpath(gdmldir, \"cms2018.gdml\"), Float32)\n[d.volume.label for d in cms[1].daughters][1:8]\n\n8-element Vector{String}:\n \"CMStoZDC0x7f4a9a83fd80\"\n \"CMStoZDC0x7f4a9a83fd80\"\n \"ZDCtoFP4200x7f4a9a83fe00\"\n \"ZDCtoFP4200x7f4a9a83fe00\"\n \"Tracker0x7f4a9a83fe80\"\n \"CALO0x7f4a9a83ff00\"\n \"MUON0x7f4a9a758080\"\n \"BEAM0x7f4a9a83ff80\"\n\nThe muon system is the 7th daughter of the CMS envelope, and the muon barrel the first daughter of the muon system. world_volume puts it into a world box of its own.\n\nbarrel = cms[1, 7, 1]\nworld = world_volume(barrel)\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 1], show_axis=false), world; maxlevel=3)\nfig\n\n<img width=800 height=600 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"The CMS detector"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/GDML/#X-ray-images","page":"GDML","text":"The X-ray images integrate the density along rays parallel to the axes, through the navigator of the closed geometry model.\n\nmodel = close_geometry(world)\n\nGeometryModel{Float32}(volumes=701, placements=6885, materials=17, shape kinds=4, shapes=438, bvh nodes=2287, csg components=87)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(1000, 500))\nfor axis in (3, 1)\n    u, v, image, nsteps = xray(model, axis, 300)\n    heatmap!(Axis(fig[1, axis == 3 ? 1 : 2], title=\"rays along $(\"xyz\"[axis])\", aspect=DataAspect()),\n             u, v, image, colormap=:grayC)\nend\nfig\n\n<img width=1000 height=500 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\nThe whole detector (world_volume(cms) instead of the barrel) can be processed in the same way. It is much faster on a GPU: see examples/XRay.jl in the repository.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"X-ray images"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#Release-Notes","page":"Release Notes","text":"","title":"Release Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#Unreleased","page":"Release Notes","text":"New features:\nMaterials as in Geant4 (G4Isotope, G4Element, G4Material): elements by Z and molar mass (with the natural isotopes) or by isotopes, materials of a single element, compounds by number of atoms (atoms=[H => 2, O => 1]) and mixtures of elements and materials by mass fractions (fractions=[...]), with state, temperature and pressure. The derived quantities are computed as in Geant4: radiation_length (Tsai), nuclear_interaction_length, electron_density, mean_excitation_energy, mass_fractions and atoms_per_volume (#27).\nNIST data of Geant4 11.3 (generated by utils/generate_nist_data.py): nist_element (the elements with their natural isotopes) and nist_material (the G4_<symbol> materials of the elements and a selection of compounds and HEP materials, e.g. G4_WATER, G4_AIR, G4_PbWO4, G4_Galactic) (#27).\nGeom4hep.Units: units of mass, amount, density, energy, temperature and pressure (with densities in g/cm³), and the physical constants of CLHEP used for the materials (#27).\nGDML: the materials are read completely, with units: elements by Z or by isotopes, materials of a single element, <composite>, <fraction> of elements and materials, <T>, <P>, <MEE> and the references to <quantity>, and the NIST elements and materials referenced but not defined in the file (#27).\nxray(...; quantity=:radlen) and quantity=:nuclen: images of the material budget in radiation or nuclear interaction lengths. The flat model (FlatMaterial) carries the radiation and interaction lengths and the electron density (#27).\nBreaking changes:\nIsotope{T}(name, Z, N, A) takes Z before N (as G4Isotope), and the molar mass is the field A (was Amass).\nElement and Material have new constructors and fields: composition is replaced by isotopes/abundances and elements/massfractions, state is a Symbol, and the Material keyword arguments mass and composition are removed (Material{T}(name; density=...) still makes a material with only a density) (#27).","title":"Unreleased"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#0.1.1-25-11-2026","page":"Release Notes","text":"New features:\nshape_to_mesh: mesh of a shape with normals at the vertices, used by draw for smooth shading of curved surfaces and sharp edges (#24).\ncsg_mesh: mesh of the boolean shapes (and MultiUnion) computed with boolean operations on the meshes of their operands (BSP trees, as csg.js and ROOT). The result is a closed mesh, with the coplanar fragments merged. Used by mesh and draw for the boolean shapes and MultiUnion, which were drawn before as the overlay of their operands (#25).\nRoundCube example: the cube with rounded edges and corners of the Geant4.jl UserLib example, built from boolean shapes, with the translation of the Geant4 transformations (#21). The Shapes and RoundCube examples draw the boolean shapes (#25).\nChanges:\ndraw and draw! pass the extra keyword arguments to the Makie plotting functions, e.g. draw(shape; color=:blue) or draw(shape; wireframe=true, linewidth=2) (#26).\nFixes:\nBoolean shapes: inside and normal at the points on the surface of both operands (seams of unions, edges where the cut of a subtraction meets the surface), and the quick bounding box checks for the points on the surface of an operand (#21).\nMaintenance:\n[compat] entries for the docs environment (CompatHelper: #18, #19, #20, #23).","title":"0.1.1 - 25-11-2026"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#0.1.0-19-11-2026","page":"Release Notes","text":"New features:\nShapes: Sphere (with ϕ and θ segments, also GDML orb) (#9), Polyhedra (#10) and Torus (#12), and the mesh of all the shapes for drawing.\nFlat geometry model (close_geometry) and navigator (locate, compute_step) shared by CPUs and GPUs (KernelAbstractions), with boolean shapes as flat CSG programs and BVHs for the daughters of volumes. xray images on CPUs and GPUs (#8).\nSingle precision (Float32) support, needed by Apple GPUs (#7, #8).\nShape benchmarks with the methodology of VecGeom (benchmark_shape, vecgeom_suite) and comparison with VecGeom (#14).\nDocumentation with Documenter and Literate examples (#15).\nChanges:\nsnake_case API (distance_to_in, read_gdml, world_volume, ...) and own point and vector types (Point3, Vector3) (#7).\nDrawing functions moved to a Makie package extension (#7).\nRepository moved to the JuliaHEP organization (#16), and prepared for registration in the General registry: license, TagBot and CompatHelper (#17).\nFixes:\nMany fixes of the shape functions at surfaces and for grazing rays (#7, #8, #9).\nNavigator: the X-ray kernel of the full CMS geometry compiles again on Metal (#11).\nTrap with a face that has an edge of zero length (CMS TEC module frames) (#13).","title":"0.1.0 - 19-11-2026"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Volumes","page":"Volumes","text":"This example builds a small detector as a hierarchy of volumes, as in Geant4: logical volumes (a shape and a material) placed inside each other with a transformation. It then draws it.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"Volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Table-of-contents","page":"Volumes","text":"Pages = [\"Volumes.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Loading-the-modules","page":"Volumes","text":"using Geom4hep, Geom4hep.Units\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, LScene\n\nconst T = Float64;","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Materials","page":"Volumes","text":"Only the density of the materials is used for now (in g/cm³, as in GDML files).\n\nvacuum = Material{T}(\"vacuum\"; density=0)\nair    = Material{T}(\"air\"; density=0.0012)\niron   = Material{T}(\"iron\"; density=7.87)\nlead   = Material{T}(\"lead\"; density=11.35)\nwater  = Material{T}(\"water\"; density=1.0);","title":"Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Volumes-2","page":"Volumes","text":"A Volume is a shape filled with a material: the logical volume of Geant4.\n\nworld  = Volume{T}(\"world\", Box{T}(1m, 1m, 1m), air)\nplate  = Volume{T}(\"plate\", Box{T}(10cm, 20cm, 30cm), iron)\nbrick  = Volume{T}(\"brick\", Box{T}(4cm, 4cm, 4cm), lead)\ntank   = Volume{T}(\"tank\", Trd{T}(25cm, 10cm, 25cm, 10cm, 10cm), water)\nball   = Volume{T}(\"ball\", Sphere{T}(0, 8cm), lead)\npipe   = Volume{T}(\"pipe\", Tube{T}(4cm, 5cm, 80cm, 0, 2π), iron);","title":"Volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Placements","page":"Volumes","text":"place_daughter!(mother, transformation, daughter) places a daughter volume in a mother volume. Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z) places it at (x, y, z) of the mother frame, and the optional rotation rotates the frame of the mother into the frame of the daughter. The same volume can be placed several times: here the plate (with a lead brick inside) is placed at the 8 corners of a cube, with different rotations.\n\nplace_daughter!(plate, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 0), brick)\nfor (i, (x, y, z)) in enumerate(Iterators.product((-50cm, 50cm), (-50cm, 50cm), (-50cm, 50cm)))\n    rotation = RotXYZ{T}(i % 3 == 0 ? π/4 : 0, i % 3 == 1 ? π/4 : 0, i % 3 == 2 ? π/4 : 0)\n    place_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z, rotation), plate)\nend\nplace_daughter!(tank, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 0), ball)\nplace_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 0), tank)\nplace_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 40cm, π/2, 0, 0), pipe) # along y, above the tank\n\nVolume{Float64} name = world\n\nPrinting a volume shows its shape, material and daughters\n\nworld\n\nVolume{Float64} name = world\n\nThe daughters of a volume are PlacedVolumes, with the volume and its transformation. Indexing a volume goes down the hierarchy: world[1] is the volume of the first daughter of the world, and world[1, 1] the volume of its first daughter.\n\nworld[1, 1]\n\nVolume{Float64} name = brick","title":"Placements"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Assemblies","page":"Volumes","text":"An Assembly groups volumes without a shape of its own: placing it places each of its volumes in the mother. Here two small iron bars are placed below the tank.\n\nbar = Volume{T}(\"bar\", Box{T}(20cm, 2cm, 2cm), iron)\ngroup = Assembly{T}(\"bars\")\nplace_daughter!(group, Transformation3D{T}(0, -5cm, 0), bar)\nplace_daughter!(group, Transformation3D{T}(0, 5cm, 0), bar)\nplace_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, -30cm), group)\nlength(world.daughters)\n\n12","title":"Assemblies"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Volumes/#Drawing","page":"Volumes","text":"draw! draws the volumes of the hierarchy with grey levels from light (outer volumes) to dark (deep daughters). The world volume itself is not drawn.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 1], show_axis=false), world)\nfig\n\n<img width=800 height=600 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\nWith wireframe=true the edges of the meshes are drawn instead, and maxlevel limits the depth.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 1], show_axis=false), world; wireframe=true, maxlevel=2)\nfig\n\n<img width=800 height=600 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Drawing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public-API","page":"Public API","text":"Documentation of the public interface of Geom4hep.jl.","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"Public API","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Points,-vectors-and-transformations","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Points, vectors and transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Shapes","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Shape-functions","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Shape functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Materials","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Volumes","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Geometry-model-and-navigation","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Geometry model and navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Visualization","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Benchmarks","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Units","page":"Public API","text":"","title":"Units"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Point3","page":"Public API","text":"Point3{T}(x, y, z)\n\nA position in 3D space. Point3 - Point3 gives a Vector3, Point3 ± Vector3 a Point3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Point3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Vector3","page":"Public API","text":"Vector3{T}(x, y, z)\n\nA displacement or direction in 3D space. Transformations rotate but do not translate vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Vector3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Point2","page":"Public API","text":"Point2{T}(x, y)\n\nA position in 2D space (used for computations in the transverse plane).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Point2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Transformation3D","page":"Public API","text":"Transformation3D{T}(dx, dy, dz)\nTransformation3D{T}(dx, dy, dz, rotation::Rotation{3})\nTransformation3D{T}(dx, dy, dz, rotx, roty, rotz)\nTransformation3D{T}(rotation::RotMatrix3, translation::Vector3)\none(Transformation3D{T})\n\nPlacement of a daughter volume (or of a boolean operand) in the frame of its mother. Applied to a point of the mother, it gives the point in the frame of the daughter: t * p == R * (p - t) for a translation t = (dx, dy, dz) and rotation R, so Transformation3D{T}(dx, dy, dz) places the daughter at (dx, dy, dz). Rotations come from Rotations.jl (RotXYZ, RotZXZ, ...); the angles rotx, roty, rotz build a RotXYZ. p * t applies the inverse transformation, and t1 * t2 composes them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Transformation3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.AbstractShape","page":"Public API","text":"AbstractShape{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nSupertype of all the shapes (solids). T is the floating point type of the shape parameters and of the points and directions it works with (Float64 or Float32). A shape is defined in its own frame; it is positioned in a mother volume by the transformation of its placement.\n\nEvery shape implements inside, distance_to_in, distance_to_out, safety_to_in, safety_to_out, normal, extent, capacity and surface, and the mesh functions used for drawing (coordinates, faces, mesh).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.AbstractShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Box","page":"Public API","text":"Box{T}(x, y, z)\n\nBox (as G4Box) centred at the origin, with half lengths x, y and z along the axes. box[i] gives the half length along axis i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Box"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Trd","page":"Public API","text":"Trd{T}(x1, x2, y1, y2, z)\n\nTrapezoid (as G4Trd) with half length z along the z axis and rectangular faces at -z and +z with half lengths (x1, y1) and (x2, y2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Trd"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Trap","page":"Public API","text":"Trap{T}(z, θ, ϕ, y1, x1, x2, α1, y2, x3, x4, α2)\nTrap{T}(x1, x2, y1, y2, z)\n\nGeneral trapezoid (as G4Trap), with half length z. The faces at -z and +z are trapezoids with sides parallel to the x axis:\n\nat -z: half length y1 along y, half lengths x1 and x2 of the sides at y = -y1 and y = +y1, and α1 the angle to the y axis of the line joining the centres of these sides;\nat +z: the same with y2, x3, x4 and α2.\n\nθ and ϕ are the polar and azimuthal angles of the line joining the centres of the two faces. The second form is a right trapezoid with rectangular faces of half lengths (x1, y1) at -z and (x2, y2) at +z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Trap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Tube","page":"Public API","text":"Tube{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)\n\nTube or tube segment (as G4Tubs) along the z axis: inner and outer radii rmin and rmax (rmin = 0 for a solid cylinder), half length z, and the ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ] (0, 2π for a full tube).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Tube"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Cone","page":"Public API","text":"Cone{T}(rmin1, rmax1, rmin2, rmax2, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)\n\nCone or cone segment (as G4Cons) along the z axis, with half length z: inner and outer radii rmin1 and rmax1 at -z, rmin2 and rmax2 at +z, and the ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Cone"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.CutTube","page":"Public API","text":"CutTube{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ, bottom_normal::Vector3, top_normal::Vector3)\nCutTube{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ, θb, ϕb, θt, ϕt)\n\nTube segment cut by two planes (as G4CutTubs): a Tube with radii rmin, rmax and ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ] whose ends are the planes through (0, 0, -z) and (0, 0, z) with outward normals bottom_normal and top_normal, given as vectors or by their polar and azimuthal angles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.CutTube"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Sphere","page":"Public API","text":"Sphere{T}(rmin, rmax, ϕ₀=0, Δϕ=2π, θ₀=0, Δθ=π)\n\nSpherical shell or section (as G4Sphere): inner and outer radii rmin and rmax, the ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ] and the polar range [θ₀, θ₀ + Δθ]. Sphere{T}(0, r) is a full ball (G4Orb).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Sphere"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Torus","page":"Public API","text":"Torus{T}(rmin, rmax, rtor, ϕ₀=0, Δϕ=2π)\n\nTorus or torus segment (as G4Torus): the points at a distance between rmin and rmax from the circle of radius rtor in the xy plane (the swept radius), within the ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Torus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Polycone","page":"Public API","text":"Polycone{T}(rmin::Vector, rmax::Vector, z::Vector, ϕ₀, Δϕ)\n\nPolycone (as G4Polycone, z-planes form): a sequence of cone sections along z. z are the z planes (increasing) and rmin, rmax the inner and outer radii at each plane; the ϕ range is [ϕ₀, ϕ₀ + Δϕ].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Polycone"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Polyhedra","page":"Public API","text":"Polyhedra{T}(nsides, rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀=0, Δϕ=2π)\n\nPolyhedra (as G4Polyhedra) with nsides sides in the ϕ range [ϕ₀, ϕ₀+Δϕ] and z planes z with inner and outer radii rmin and rmax. The radii are the distances from the z axis to the sides.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Polyhedra"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.BooleanUnion","page":"Public API","text":"BooleanUnion(left, right, placement=one(Transformation3D{T}))\nBooleanSubtraction(left, right, placement=one(Transformation3D{T}))\nBooleanIntersection(left, right, placement=one(Transformation3D{T}))\n\nBoolean combination of two shapes (as G4UnionSolid, G4SubtractionSolid and G4IntersectionSolid): the points in left or right, in left and not in right, or in both. right is placed in the frame of left with placement (as in place_daughter!). The operands can themselves be boolean shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.BooleanUnion"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.BooleanSubtraction","page":"Public API","text":"See BooleanUnion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.BooleanSubtraction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.BooleanIntersection","page":"Public API","text":"See BooleanUnion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.BooleanIntersection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.MultiUnion","page":"Public API","text":"MultiUnion{T}(shapes, transformations)\nMultiUnion{T}()\n\nUnion of any number of shapes (as G4MultiUnion), each one placed with its transformation (as in place_daughter!). Components can be added with add_component!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.MultiUnion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.add_component!","page":"Public API","text":"add_component!(mu::MultiUnion, shape, transformation=one(Transformation3D{T}))\n\nAdd a component to a MultiUnion built by the user.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.add_component!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.NoShape","page":"Public API","text":"NoShape{T}()\n\nPlaceholder for a shape that is not supported (e.g. read from GDML), and the shape of an Assembly. It contains no points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.NoShape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.inside","page":"Public API","text":"inside(shape, point) -> kInside | kSurface | kOutside\n\nClassify point (in the frame of shape) as inside the shape, on its surface (within tolerance) or outside it. The constants kInside, kSurface and kOutside are exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.inside"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.distance_to_in","page":"Public API","text":"distance_to_in(shape, point, dir)\n\nDistance along the unit direction dir from point, outside shape, to where the ray enters the shape. Inf if the ray misses the shape. A point on the surface moving into the shape gets 0. The result is not meaningful for points inside the shape (see inside).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.distance_to_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.distance_to_out","page":"Public API","text":"distance_to_out(shape, point, dir)\n\nDistance along the unit direction dir from point, inside shape, to where the ray leaves the shape. A point on the surface moving out of the shape gets 0, and a point outside the shape gets a negative value (-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.distance_to_out"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.safety_to_in","page":"Public API","text":"safety_to_in(shape, point)\n\nLower bound of the distance from point, outside shape, to the shape (an isotropic safe step). It is cheaper to compute than the exact distance; it is ≤ 0 for points inside the shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.safety_to_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.safety_to_out","page":"Public API","text":"safety_to_out(shape, point)\n\nLower bound of the distance from point, inside shape, to its surface (an isotropic safe step). It is ≤ 0 for points outside the shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.safety_to_out"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.normal","page":"Public API","text":"normal(shape, point)\n\nUnit outward normal of shape at point. On edges and corners the normals of the surfaces meeting there are averaged. Returns the zero vector if point is not on the surface (within tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.normal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.extent","page":"Public API","text":"extent(shape) -> (lower::Point3, upper::Point3)\n\nCorners of an axis-aligned box, in the frame of the shape, that contains it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.extent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.capacity","page":"Public API","text":"capacity(shape)\n\nVolume of the shape. For shapes without a closed formula it is estimated by sampling (see estimate_capacity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.surface","page":"Public API","text":"surface(shape)\n\nArea of the surface of the shape. For shapes without a closed formula it is estimated by sampling (see estimate_surface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.surface"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.estimate_capacity","page":"Public API","text":"estimate_capacity(shape; npoints=1_000_000)\n\nVolume of shape estimated from the fraction of npoints sample points of its bounding box that are inside it. Typical accuracy with the default number of points is better than 0.1%.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.estimate_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.estimate_surface","page":"Public API","text":"estimate_surface(shape; npoints=1_000_000)\n\nSurface area of shape estimated by sampling segments of length δ with isotropic directions in its (enlarged) bounding box of volume V: the probability that a segment crosses the surface is A δ / 2V. Typical accuracy with the default number of points is about 1% (slightly underestimated).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.estimate_surface"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.tolerance","page":"Public API","text":"tolerance(T=Float64)\n\nThickness of the surfaces of the shapes: a point closer than tolerance(T)/2 to a surface is on the surface (inside returns kSurface). 1 nm for Float64 and 1 µm for Float32 (lengths in mm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.tolerance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.push_tolerance","page":"Public API","text":"push_tolerance(T=Float64)\n\nDistance by which the navigator pushes a point beyond a boundary it has reached, to locate it in the next volume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.push_tolerance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.AbstractMaterial","page":"Public API","text":"AbstractMaterial{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nSupertype of Isotope, Element and Material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.AbstractMaterial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Isotope","page":"Public API","text":"Isotope{T}(name, Z, N, A=0; m=0)\n\nIsotope with atomic number Z, number of nucleons N, molar mass A (e.g. A=55.935g/mole) and isomer level m (as G4Isotope). When A is 0, the mass of the isotope is taken from the NIST data (natural isotopes only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Isotope"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Element","page":"Public API","text":"Element{T}(name, symbol, Z, A)\nElement{T}(name, symbol, isotopes, abundances)\n\nChemical element (as G4Element), given by its effective atomic number Z and molar mass A (e.g. Element{T}(\"Iron\", \"Fe\", 26, 55.845g/mole)), or by its isotopes and their relative abundances in number of atoms. In the first case, the isotopes are the natural ones of the NIST data.\n\nThe element holds the derived quantities of Geant4: the effective number of nucleons N (A/(g/mole)), the Coulomb correction fcoulomb and the Tsai factor radtsai of the radiation length (per atom, in mm²), and the mean excitation energy I of the NIST data. See also nist_element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Element"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Material","page":"Public API","text":"Material{T}(name, Z, A, density; state=:undefined, temperature=NTP_Temperature, pressure=STP_Pressure, I=0)\nMaterial{T}(name, density; atoms=[...] or fractions=[...], state=:undefined, temperature=NTP_Temperature, pressure=STP_Pressure, I=0, formula=\"\")\nMaterial{T}(name; density=0, state=:undefined, temperature=NTP_Temperature, pressure=STP_Pressure)\n\nMaterial of a volume (as G4Material), with the quantities in the units of Geom4hep.Units (the density in g/cm³, e.g. 7.874g/cm3 or 7.874).\n\nA material of a single element of atomic number Z and molar mass A (the element is the NIST one for Z, as in Geant4).\nA compound given by the number of atoms of each element in a molecule, atoms=[element => n, ...] (as G4Material::AddElementByNumberOfAtoms), e.g. atoms=[H => 2, O => 1].\nA mixture given by mass fractions of elements and materials, fractions=[element_or_material => w, ...] (as AddElementByMassFraction and AddMaterial), e.g. fractions=[N => 0.755, O => 0.232, Ar => 0.013] or fractions=[water => 0.5, lead => 0.5]. The fractions are normalized, with a warning when their sum differs from 1 by more than 0.1%.\nA material with only a density (no composition), e.g. for the navigation or xray with the mass thickness: its radiation and interaction lengths are NaN (Inf if the density is 0).\n\nstate is :solid, :liquid, :gas or :undefined (solid above 10 mg/cm³, gas below). I is the mean excitation energy; when it is 0, it is computed from the ones of the elements (as G4IonisParamMat). The derived quantities are computed as in Geant4: see radiation_length, nuclear_interaction_length, electron_density, mean_excitation_energy, mass_fractions and atoms_per_volume. See also nist_material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Material"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.nist_element","page":"Public API","text":"nist_element([T=Float64,] symbol_or_Z)\n\nElement of the NIST data with its natural isotopic composition (as G4NistManager::FindOrBuildElement), given by its symbol (e.g. \"Fe\") or atomic number. The elements are built once and shared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.nist_element"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.nist_material","page":"Public API","text":"nist_material([T=Float64,] name)\n\nMaterial of the NIST data of Geant4 (as G4NistManager::FindOrBuildMaterial), e.g. \"G4_WATER\", \"G4_AIR\", \"G4_Si\" or \"G4_PbWO4\": the simple materials G4_<symbol> of the elements, and a selection of compounds and HEP materials (see nist_material_names). The materials are built once and shared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.nist_material"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.nist_material_names","page":"Public API","text":"Names of the materials available with nist_material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.nist_material_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.radiation_length","page":"Public API","text":"Radiation length of a Material (mm), with Tsai's formula as in Geant4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.radiation_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.nuclear_interaction_length","page":"Public API","text":"Nuclear interaction length of a Material (mm), with the approximation of Geant4 (35 g/cm² × A^(2/3) per atom).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.nuclear_interaction_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.electron_density","page":"Public API","text":"Number of electrons per volume (1/mm³) of a Material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.electron_density"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.mean_excitation_energy","page":"Public API","text":"Mean excitation energy (MeV) of a Material or an Element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.mean_excitation_energy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.mass_fractions","page":"Public API","text":"Mass fractions of the elements of a Material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.mass_fractions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.atoms_per_volume","page":"Public API","text":"Number of atoms per volume (1/mm³) of each element of a Material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.atoms_per_volume"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Volume","page":"Public API","text":"Volume{T}(label, shape, material)\n\nA logical volume (as G4LogicalVolume): a shape filled with a material, with daughter volumes placed inside it with place_daughter!. The same volume can be placed several times. vol[i] gives the volume of the i-th daughter, and vol[i, j, ...] goes down the hierarchy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Volume"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.PlacedVolume","page":"Public API","text":"PlacedVolume{T}\n\nA daughter volume placed in its mother: the volume and its transformation (see place_daughter!). The daughters of a Volume are in its daughters vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.PlacedVolume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.place_daughter!","page":"Public API","text":"place_daughter!(mother::Volume, placement::Transformation3D, daughter::Volume)\n\nPlace daughter inside mother with placement (see Transformation3D: Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z) places the daughter at (x, y, z) of the mother). The daughter must be contained in the mother and must not overlap the other daughters (this is not checked). Placing an Assembly places each of its volumes. Returns mother.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.place_daughter!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Assembly","page":"Public API","text":"Assembly{T}(label)\n\nA group of volumes without a shape of its own (as G4AssemblyVolume): its daughters are placed directly in the mother when the assembly is placed with place_daughter!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Assembly"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.world_volume","page":"Public API","text":"world_volume(vol::Volume)\n\nvol if its label is \"world\"; otherwise a new world volume (a box of vacuum 5% larger than vol) with vol placed in it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.world_volume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.read_gdml","page":"Public API","text":"read_gdml(filename, T=Float64) -> Volume{T}\n\nRead a GDML file and return its world volume, with the shapes, materials and volume hierarchy it defines, with floating point type T. Lengths are converted to mm and angles to radians. Solids that are not supported are replaced by NoShape with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.read_gdml"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.close_geometry","page":"Public API","text":"close_geometry(world::Volume; bvh_threshold=4, with_index=false)\n\nBuild a flat GeometryModel from a hierarchy of volumes. Boolean shapes are converted to CSG programs over primitive shapes, and a BVH is built for the daughters of volumes with more than bvh_threshold daughters.\n\nWith with_index=true it returns (model, index), where index.volumes[i] is the Volume stored as volume i of the model (the volume field of a NavState) and index.materials[i] the Material with index i (see material_index). This allows to attach information to the volumes and materials of the model, e.g. by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.close_geometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.GeometryModel","page":"Public API","text":"GeometryModel\n\nFlat representation of a geometry, built with close_geometry. D is 1 if the model has boolean (CSG) shapes, 0 otherwise. Use Adapt.adapt(backend, model) to move it to a GPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.GeometryModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.ShapeRef","page":"Public API","text":"Reference to a shape of a GeometryModel: kind (index in the tuple of shape arrays) and index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.ShapeRef"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.shape_call","page":"Public API","text":"shape_call(f, model::GeometryModel, ref::ShapeRef, args...)\n\nCall the shape function f (e.g. inside, distance_to_in, extent) on the shape ref of model without dynamic dispatch: shape_call(inside, model, model.volumes[1].shape, point).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.shape_call"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.NavState","page":"Public API","text":"NavState{T}\n\nPosition of a point in the volume hierarchy of a GeometryModel: the current volume, the path of placements from the world (depth levels) and the transformation from the global frame to the frame of the current volume. volume == 0 when the point is outside the world. blocked is the daughter (placement) of the current volume that has just been left, if any (see compute_step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.NavState"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.locate","page":"Public API","text":"locate(model::GeometryModel, point) -> NavState\n\nFind the deepest volume containing the (global) point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.locate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.compute_step","page":"Public API","text":"compute_step(model::GeometryModel, state, point, dir, step_limit) -> (step, new_state)\n\nDistance from the global point along dir to the next boundary (or step_limit), and the state after crossing it. The new state is computed for the point pushed slightly beyond the boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.compute_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.in_world","page":"Public API","text":"in_world(state::NavState)\n\ntrue if the point of state is inside the world volume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.in_world"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.material_index","page":"Public API","text":"material_index(model::GeometryModel, state::NavState)\n\nIndex in model.materials of the material of the current volume of state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.material_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.xray","page":"Public API","text":"xray(model::GeometryModel, axis, npixels; quantity=:mass, backend=CPU(), maxsteps=10_000, launch_time=0.5)\n\nX-ray image of the world volume of model with rays parallel to axis (1, 2 or 3 for x, y, z): the quantity integrated along each ray,\n\n:mass: the mass thickness (density × length, in g/cm³ × mm);\n:radlen: the material budget in radiation lengths (length/X0);\n:nuclen: the material budget in nuclear interaction lengths (length/λ).\n\nThe materials without composition (only a density) have unknown X0 and λ, which make the pixels that cross them NaN (the ones with zero density, e.g. a vacuum, do not contribute). npixels is the number of pixels along the first transverse axis. Returns (u, v, image, nsteps) with the pixel centres along the two transverse axes, the image and the number of navigation steps per pixel.\n\nbackend is a KernelAbstractions backend: CPU() (Julia threads), or a GPU backend such as MetalBackend() or CUDABackend() (the model is copied to the device). On GPUs the image is computed in several kernel launches, each one taking about launch_time seconds: a long-running kernel on a GPU that also drives a display is aborted by the operating system (e.g. macOS error \"Impacting Interactivity\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.xray"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.draw","page":"Public API","text":"draw(object; kwargs...)\n\nDraw a shape, volume hierarchy or BVH in a new figure. Requires a Makie backend (e.g. using GLMakie). The keyword arguments are:\n\nwireframe=false: draw the edges of the faces instead of the surfaces (shapes and volumes);\nbvh=false and maxlevel=999: draw the bounding boxes of the daughters, and the depth of the volume hierarchy to draw (volumes);\nany other keyword argument is passed to the Makie plotting function (mesh! or wireframe!), and overrides the defaults, e.g. draw(shape; color=:blue), draw(shape; color=(:red, 0.5)) or draw(shape; wireframe=true, linewidth=2). For a volume, it applies to all the levels of the hierarchy (the default colors are grey levels by depth).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.draw"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.draw!","page":"Public API","text":"draw!(scene, object; kwargs...)\n\nDraw a shape, volume hierarchy, AABB or BVH into an existing LScene. Requires a Makie backend. The keyword arguments are as for draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.draw!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.draw_distance_to_in","page":"Public API","text":"draw_distance_to_in(shape, npoints)\n\nFigure with the points where random rays from outside shape enter it (a test of distance_to_in). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.draw_distance_to_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.draw_distance_to_out","page":"Public API","text":"draw_distance_to_out(shape, npoints)\n\nFigure with the points where random rays from inside shape leave it (a test of distance_to_out). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.draw_distance_to_out"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometryBasics.mesh","page":"Public API","text":"mesh(shape::AbstractShape; facets=64)\n\nSurface mesh of a shape (GeometryBasics.Mesh), e.g. for visualization. facets controls the number of segments used to approximate curved surfaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometryBasics.mesh"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.shape_to_mesh","page":"Public API","text":"shape_to_mesh(shape; facets=64, transformation=one(Transformation3D{T}), crease_angle=π/6)\n\nSurface mesh of shape (a GeometryBasics.Mesh of triangles) with per-vertex normals, for smooth shading of curved surfaces and sharp shading of edges.\n\nThe mesh of mesh is processed as follows:\n\nduplicated vertices are merged, and the faces are split into triangles;\nthe triangles are oriented outwards, using the normal of the shape at their vertices (and, where the vertices are not on the surface, their neighbouring triangles);\nthe normal of a vertex is the average of the normals of the triangles around it that make an angle smaller than crease_angle with each other. A vertex on a hard edge is duplicated, one copy for each side of the edge. When the normal of the shape at a vertex agrees with this average, it is used instead, which is exact on curved surfaces.\n\nfacets is the number of segments of the curved surfaces, and transformation is applied to the mesh (it places the shape in the frame of its mother, as in place_daughter!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.shape_to_mesh"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.csg_mesh","page":"Public API","text":"csg_mesh(shape; facets=64, eps=1e-7)\n\nSurface mesh of a boolean shape (or a MultiUnion) computed with boolean operations on the meshes of its operands (BSP trees, as csg.js). eps is the tolerance to classify vertices on the planes of the polygons, relative to the size of the shape.\n\nThe polygons of the result are cleaned up:\n\nthe vertices are welded, and the T-junctions (vertices of a polygon in the middle of an edge of its neighbour, left by the splits) are removed by inserting these vertices in the edges;\nthe connected coplanar fragments are merged into faces (with holes), and the vertices in the middle of straight edges are removed, and the faces are triangulated again.\n\nThe mesh is closed: every edge is shared by two triangles, in opposite directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.csg_mesh"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Geom4hep.ShapeBenchmark","page":"Public API","text":"ShapeBenchmark{T}(name, shape, world, placement, npoints, nrep, vecgeom)\n\nA benchmark case: a shape placed with placement (a Transformation3D as given to place_daughter!: Transformation3D(x, y, z) puts the shape at (x, y, z)) in a world box of half lengths world, timed for npoints points and nrep repetitions. vecgeom is the VecGeom benchmark program (and options) with the same setup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.ShapeBenchmark"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.benchmark_shape","page":"Public API","text":"benchmark_shape(shape; world, placement, npoints=1024, nrep=4, inside_bias=0.5, toin_bias=0.8,\n                modes=(:shape, :model), seed=1, verbose=true)\nbenchmark_shape(b::ShapeBenchmark; kwargs...)\n\nTime the shape functions as the shape benchmarks of VecGeom: inside, contains, distance_to_in, safety_to_in, distance_to_out and safety_to_out (see the header of Benchmark.jl). world are the half lengths of the world box (default: 4 times the extent of the shape) and placement the placement of the shape in it (as for place_daughter!). Returns a vector of named tuples (case, T, mode, op, npoints, nrep, seconds, ns), with the total time and the time per call in ns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.benchmark_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Geom4hep.vecgeom_suite","page":"Public API","text":"vecgeom_suite(T=Float64)\n\nThe shape benchmarks of VecGeom (test/benchmark/*Benchmark.cpp) for the shapes of Geom4hep, with the same shape parameters, world box, placement, number of points and repetitions as the defaults of the VecGeom programs. Not reproduced: the corner-defined Trapezoid (type 1) and the (r,z) Polycone (type 4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.vecgeom_suite"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Geom4hep.Units","page":"Public API","text":"Geom4hep.Units\n\nSystem of units, with the conventions of Geant4 (CLHEP) for lengths (mm), angles (radian), energies (MeV) and temperatures (kelvin). Masses are in mg, so that densities are in g/cm³ (g/cm3 == 1) and molar masses in mg/mole (g/mole == 1000). Use as using Geom4hep.Units; Box{Float64}(1cm, 2cm, 3cm) or density = 7.87g/cm3.\n\nIt also defines the physical constants used for the materials: Avogadro, k_Boltzmann, amu, fine_structure_const, classic_electr_radius, the standard temperature and pressure (STP_Temperature, NTP_Temperature, STP_Pressure) and universe_mean_density, with the values of CLHEP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Geom4hep.Units"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Structure-of-the-package","page":"Development","text":"Directory Content\nsrc/shapes One file per shape: its type, the shape functions and the mesh for drawing\nsrc/model The flat GeometryModel: its types, the CSG programs of boolean shapes and close_geometry\nsrc/navigation The navigator (locate, compute_step) and xray\nsrc/GDML.jl The GDML reader\nsrc/Benchmark.jl The shape benchmarks\next/ The Makie extension (drawing)\ntest/ The unit tests\nexamples/ Examples with their own environment, and GDML files\ndocs/ This documentation","title":"Structure of the package"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Running-the-tests","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=. -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Building-the-documentation","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe examples are converted from their Literate sources (docs/src/examples/*.lit) and executed by docs/make.jl; the generated pages, notebooks and scripts are not committed. Open docs/build/index.html to see the result. The documentation is built and deployed by the CI for each push to master and for each pull request (as a preview).","title":"Building the documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Adding-a-shape","page":"Development","text":"Add the shape type and its functions (inside, distance_to_in, distance_to_out, safety_to_in, safety_to_out, normal, extent, capacity, surface, and coordinates and faces for the mesh) in a new file in src/shapes, and include it in src/Geom4hep.jl.\nAdd it to BaseShape in src/shapes/Boolean.jl, and to the flat model in src/model/GeometryModel.jl and src/model/CloseGeometry.jl. Shapes with variable-size data (as polycones) keep it in shared arrays of the model.\nRead it from GDML in src/GDML.jl.\nAdd tests, comparing with Geant4 or VecGeom where possible, also in Float32.\nCheck the size of the GPU kernels: Apple's compiler inlines all calls, and fails (or takes very long) on too large kernels. Avoid adding call sites of the shape functions and unrolled loops that call them (see the @nounroll macro).","title":"Adding a shape"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Navigation","page":"Navigation","text":"This example closes a geometry into a flat GeometryModel, follows rays through it with the navigator, and makes X-ray images of it. The same navigator runs on GPUs.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"Navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Table-of-contents","page":"Navigation","text":"Pages = [\"Navigation.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Loading-the-modules","page":"Navigation","text":"using Geom4hep, Geom4hep.Units\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, Axis, heatmap!, lines!, Colorbar","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#A-geometry","page":"Navigation","text":"A box of water with a lead ball inside, a steel shell around it, and a hole along z. The materials are taken from the NIST data of Geant4.\n\nfunction build(T)\n    world = Volume{T}(\"world\", Box{T}(50cm, 50cm, 50cm), nist_material(T, \"G4_AIR\"))\n    shell = Volume{T}(\"shell\", Sphere{T}(36cm, 38cm), nist_material(T, \"G4_STAINLESS-STEEL\"))\n    tank  = Volume{T}(\"tank\", BooleanSubtraction(Box{T}(20cm, 20cm, 20cm), Tube{T}(0, 3cm, 25cm, 0, 2π)),\n                      nist_material(T, \"G4_WATER\"))\n    ball  = Volume{T}(\"ball\", Sphere{T}(0, 8cm), nist_material(T, \"G4_Pb\"))\n    place_daughter!(tank, Transformation3D{T}(12cm, 0, 0), ball)\n    place_daughter!(world, one(Transformation3D{T}), shell)\n    place_daughter!(world, one(Transformation3D{T}), tank)\n    return world\nend\nworld = build(Float64);","title":"A geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Closing-the-geometry","page":"Navigation","text":"close_geometry converts the hierarchy into a GeometryModel: flat arrays of plain structures, shapes called without dynamic dispatch and boolean shapes as CSG programs. With with_index=true it also returns the Volume and Material of each index of the model, to get their names.\n\nmodel, index = close_geometry(world; with_index=true)\nmodel\n\nGeometryModel{Float64}(volumes=4, placements=3, materials=4, shape kinds=4, shapes=6, bvh nodes=0, csg components=2)","title":"Closing the geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Locating-points","page":"Navigation","text":"locate gives the navigation state of a point: the deepest volume that contains it, and the path of placements to it.\n\nname(state) = in_world(state) ? index.volumes[state.volume].label : \"outside\"\n[name(locate(model, Point3(x, 0.0, 0.0))) for x in (0.0, 12cm, 25cm, 37cm, 45cm, 60cm)]\n\n6-element Vector{String}:\n \"world\"\n \"ball\"\n \"world\"\n \"shell\"\n \"world\"\n \"outside\"","title":"Locating points"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Following-a-ray","page":"Navigation","text":"compute_step gives the distance to the next boundary along a direction, and the state after crossing it. Repeating it until the ray leaves the world gives the volumes it crosses.\n\nfunction follow(model, point, dir)\n    state = locate(model, point)\n    crossings = []\n    while in_world(state)\n        step, next = compute_step(model, state, point, dir, Inf)\n        push!(crossings, (volume=name(state), step=step))\n        point += step * dir\n        state = next\n    end\n    return crossings\nend\nfollow(model, Point3(-49cm, 1cm, 0.0), Vector3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0))\n\n11-element Vector{Any}:\n (volume = \"world\", step = 110.13160173554844)\n (volume = \"shell\", step = 20.007313955519948)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 159.8610843089316)\n (volume = \"tank\", step = 171.7157287525381)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 56.56854249492381)\n (volume = \"tank\", step = 12.343189420600378)\n (volume = \"ball\", step = 158.74507866387543)\n (volume = \"tank\", step = 0.6274606680622696)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 159.86108430893162)\n (volume = \"shell\", step = 20.007313955520004)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 120.13160173554843)\n\nAlong z the ray goes through the hole of the tank:\n\nfollow(model, Point3(0.0, 0.0, -49cm), Vector3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0))\n\n5-element Vector{Any}:\n (volume = \"world\", step = 110.0)\n (volume = \"shell\", step = 20.0)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 720.0)\n (volume = \"shell\", step = 20.0)\n (volume = \"world\", step = 120.0)","title":"Following a ray"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#X-ray-images","page":"Navigation","text":"xray integrates the density along parallel rays (the mass thickness), on the CPU with Julia threads or on a GPU. Here the rays are parallel to the z axis (axis 3) and to the x axis (axis 1).\n\nu, v, image, nsteps = xray(model, 3, 300)\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], aspect=1, title=\"along z\", xlabel=\"x (mm)\", ylabel=\"y (mm)\")\nhm = heatmap!(ax, u, v, image, colormap=:grays)\nu, v, image, nsteps = xray(model, 1, 300)\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2], aspect=1, title=\"along x\", xlabel=\"y (mm)\", ylabel=\"z (mm)\")\nheatmap!(ax, u, v, image, colormap=:grays)\nColorbar(fig[1, 3], hm, label=\"mass thickness (g/cm³ × mm)\")\nfig\n\n<img width=900 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"X-ray images"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Material-budget","page":"Navigation","text":"With quantity=:radlen or :nuclen, xray integrates the length in units of the radiation length X0 or of the nuclear interaction length λ of the materials: the material budget seen by particles crossing the detector. The lead ball is many radiation lengths thick, but less than one interaction length.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 400))\nfor (k, (q, label)) in enumerate(((:radlen, \"x/X0\"), (:nuclen, \"x/λ\")))\n    u, v, budget, _ = xray(model, 3, 300; quantity=q)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 2k-1], aspect=1, title=\"$label along z\", xlabel=\"x (mm)\", ylabel=\"y (mm)\")\n    hm = heatmap!(ax, u, v, budget, colormap=:viridis)\n    Colorbar(fig[1, 2k], hm, label=label)\nend\nfig\n\n<img width=900 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Material budget"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Navigation/#Single-precision-and-GPUs","page":"Navigation","text":"The same geometry in single precision gives the same results within the precision of Float32.\n\nmodel32 = close_geometry(build(Float32))\nu, v, image32, _ = xray(model32, 1, 300)\nmaximum(abs, image32 - image) / maximum(image)\n\n1.8529423483488302e-5\n\nTo run on a GPU, load its KernelAbstractions backend and give it to xray; the model is copied to the GPU (Apple GPUs need Float32):\n\nusing Metal\nu, v, image, nsteps = xray(model32, 1, 1000; backend=MetalBackend())\n\nThe navigator functions (locate, compute_step) can also be called from your own kernels: the model is a plain isbits structure once adapted to the GPU with Adapt.adapt(backend, model).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Single precision and GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#User-Guide","page":"User Guide","text":"This guide describes how to build a geometry with Geom4hep.jl and how to navigate it. The concepts are those of the Geant4 geometry (solids, logical volumes and placements); the Geometry chapter of the Geant4 Application Developer Guide describes them in more detail. The Examples show all of this running, with their output.","title":"User Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Floating-point-type-and-units","page":"User Guide","text":"All the types are parametric on the floating point type T, Float64 or Float32, which is given explicitly when creating objects: Box{Float32}(1, 2, 3). The parameters are converted to T. A geometry must use a single type; read_gdml takes it as an argument.\n\nFloat32 is needed on most GPUs (Apple GPUs do not support Float64). With Float32, the surfaces are thicker (tolerance is 1 µm instead of 1 nm), so that rounding errors do not let a ray cross a surface without seeing it.\n\nLengths are in mm and angles in radians. The module Geom4hep.Units defines units with the Geant4 conventions:\n\nusing Geom4hep, Geom4hep.Units\nbox  = Box{Float64}(1cm, 2cm, 3cm)          # half lengths 10, 20 and 30 mm\ntube = Tube{Float64}(0, 5cm, 1m, 0, 90deg)  # a quarter of a cylinder\n\nFor the materials, energies are in MeV (eV, keV, MeV, GeV), temperatures in kelvin and pressures in pascal, bar or atmosphere, as in Geant4. Masses are in mg, so that densities are in g/cm³ (7.874g/cm3 == 7.874) and molar masses in mg/mole (55.845g/mole). The module also has the physical constants used for the materials (Avogadro, amu, fine_structure_const, classic_electr_radius, STP_Pressure, NTP_Temperature, ...), with the values of CLHEP.","title":"Floating point type and units"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Points,-vectors-and-transformations","page":"User Guide","text":"Point3 is a position and Vector3 a displacement or a direction. Both are static vectors (from StaticArrays.jl), so they support indexing, broadcasting, dot, cross, norm, ... The difference of two points is a vector, and a point plus a vector is a point. Directions given to the shape functions must be unit vectors.\n\np = Point3{Float64}(1, 2, 3)\nd = normalize(Vector3{Float64}(1, 1, 0))     # using LinearAlgebra\nq = p + 10 * d                               # a Point3\n\nA Transformation3D places a daughter volume in its mother (or the right operand of a boolean shape in the frame of the left one). Applied to a point of the mother frame, it gives the point in the frame of the daughter: t * p is R * (p - t) for a translation t and rotation R. Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z) therefore places the daughter at (x, y, z). Rotations come from Rotations.jl:\n\nt1 = Transformation3D{Float64}(10, 0, 0)                          # translation\nt2 = Transformation3D{Float64}(0, 0, 50, RotXYZ{Float64}(0, 0, π/4)) # rotation and translation\nt3 = Transformation3D{Float64}(0, 0, 0, π/2, 0, 0)               # RotXYZ angles\np * t1      # inverse transformation: from the daughter frame to the mother frame\nt1 * t2     # composition\n\nNote the convention, the same as Geant4 and VecGeom use internally: the rotation R rotates points of the mother frame into the daughter frame, so the daughter appears rotated by the inverse of R.","title":"Points, vectors and transformations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Shapes","page":"User Guide","text":"A shape (solid) is defined in its own frame, usually centred at the origin. The parameters are the same as those of the corresponding Geant4 solid (half lengths, radii, and ϕ and θ ranges with a start angle and an extent).","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Supported-shapes","page":"User Guide","text":"The Geant4 solids and their status in Geom4hep (✅ supported, ❌ not yet supported):\n\nGeant4 solid Geom4hep GDML element Notes\nG4Box ✅ Box box \nG4Tubs ✅ Tube tube \nG4CutTubs ✅ CutTube cutTube \nG4Cons ✅ Cone cone \nG4Trd ✅ Trd trd \nG4Trap ✅ Trap trap \nG4Sphere ✅ Sphere sphere with ϕ and θ segments\nG4Orb ✅ Sphere orb as a full Sphere\nG4Torus ✅ Torus torus \nG4Polycone ✅ Polycone polycone z-planes form only\nG4Polyhedra ✅ Polyhedra polyhedra z-planes form only\nG4UnionSolid, G4SubtractionSolid, G4IntersectionSolid ✅ BooleanUnion, BooleanSubtraction, BooleanIntersection union, subtraction, intersection safeties are conservative (0) in the flat model\nG4MultiUnion ✅ MultiUnion ❌ multiUnion no acceleration structure over the components\nG4Para ❌ para \nG4GenericTrap ❌ arb8 \nG4GenericPolycone ❌ genericPolycone \nG4EllipticalTube ❌ eltube \nG4EllipticalCone ❌ elcone \nG4Ellipsoid ❌ ellipsoid \nG4Paraboloid ❌ paraboloid \nG4Hype ❌ hype \nG4Tet ❌ tet \nG4ExtrudedSolid ❌ xtru \nG4TessellatedSolid ❌ tessellated \nG4TwistedBox, G4TwistedTrd, G4TwistedTrap, G4TwistedTubs ❌ twistedbox, ... \nG4ScaledSolid ❌ scaledSolid \nG4ReflectedSolid ❌ reflectedSolid \n\nThe constructors:\n\nT = Float64\nBox{T}(x, y, z)                                   # half lengths\nTube{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)                    # z is the half length\nCone{T}(rmin1, rmax1, rmin2, rmax2, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)    # radii at -z and +z\nCutTube{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ, bottom_normal, top_normal)\nTrd{T}(x1, x2, y1, y2, z)                         # half lengths at -z and +z\nTrap{T}(z, θ, ϕ, y1, x1, x2, α1, y2, x3, x4, α2)  # as G4Trap\nSphere{T}(rmin, rmax, ϕ₀, Δϕ, θ₀, Δθ)\nTorus{T}(rmin, rmax, rtor, ϕ₀, Δϕ)\nPolycone{T}(rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)                # vectors of radii at the z planes\nPolyhedra{T}(nsides, rmin, rmax, z, ϕ₀, Δϕ)       # radii to the sides (apothems)","title":"Supported shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Shape-functions","page":"User Guide","text":"Every shape implements the functions that a particle transport needs. The points and directions are in the frame of the shape:\n\nFunction Result\ninside(shape, p) kInside, kSurface or kOutside\ndistance_to_in(shape, p, d) distance along d from an outside point to the shape (Inf if it misses it)\ndistance_to_out(shape, p, d) distance along d from an inside point to the surface\nsafety_to_in(shape, p) lower bound of the distance from an outside point to the shape\nsafety_to_out(shape, p) lower bound of the distance from an inside point to the surface\nnormal(shape, p) outward unit normal at a point of the surface\nextent(shape) (lower, upper) corners of a bounding box\ncapacity(shape), surface(shape) volume and surface area\n\nThe surfaces have a thickness: a point closer than tolerance(T)/2 to a surface is on it. A point on the surface moving into the shape gets distance_to_in == 0, and moving out of it distance_to_out == 0, so that a navigator that stops on a surface does not get stuck there. The safeties are cheaper than the exact distances: they are used to take steps in all directions (e.g. for multiple scattering) without computing intersections.","title":"Shape functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Boolean-shapes","page":"User Guide","text":"BooleanUnion, BooleanSubtraction and BooleanIntersection combine two shapes; the right one is placed in the frame of the left one. They can be nested:\n\nbox  = Box{T}(10, 10, 10)\nhole = Tube{T}(0, 5, 20, 0, 2π)\ns1 = BooleanSubtraction(box, hole)                                        # a box with a hole along z\ns2 = BooleanUnion(s1, Sphere{T}(0, 5), Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 10))     # and a dome on top\n\nA MultiUnion is the union of any number of placed shapes:\n\nmu = MultiUnion{T}()\nfor i in 1:10\n    add_component!(mu, Box{T}(1, 1, 1), Transformation3D{T}(3i, 0, 0))\nend","title":"Boolean shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Materials","page":"User Guide","text":"Materials follow the Geant4 model: an Isotope (G4Isotope) has an atomic number, a number of nucleons and a molar mass; an Element (G4Element) is a mixture of isotopes; a Material (G4Material) is made of elements, with a density, a state, a temperature and a pressure. The NIST data of Geant4 give the elements with their natural isotopic composition (nist_element), and the materials G4_<symbol> of all the elements and a selection of compounds and HEP materials (nist_material, nist_material_names):\n\nusing Geom4hep.Units\nwater = nist_material(T, \"G4_WATER\")\nlead  = nist_material(T, \"G4_Pb\")\nFe    = nist_element(T, \"Fe\")\n\nNew materials are made as in Geant4: of a single element, of elements given by their number of atoms in a molecule (a compound), or of elements and materials given by mass fractions (a mixture):\n\nH, O, N, Ar = (nist_element(T, s) for s in (\"H\", \"O\", \"N\", \"Ar\"))\nsilicon = Material{T}(\"Silicon\", 14, 28.0855g/mole, 2.33g/cm3)\nwater   = Material{T}(\"Water\", 1.0g/cm3; atoms=[H => 2, O => 1], state=:liquid, I=78eV)\nair     = Material{T}(\"Air\", 1.205mg/cm3; fractions=[N => 0.7553, O => 0.2318, Ar => 0.0129], state=:gas)\nmixture = Material{T}(\"WaterLead\", 6.0g/cm3; fractions=[water => 0.5, lead => 0.5])\niron    = Element{T}(\"Iron\", \"Fe\", 26, 55.845g/mole)             # with the natural isotopes\n\nThe derived quantities are computed as in Geant4: radiation_length (Tsai's formula), nuclear_interaction_length, electron_density, mean_excitation_energy (from the NIST data, from GDML, or from the elements), mass_fractions and atoms_per_volume:\n\nradiation_length(lead) / cm                # 0.5613\nnuclear_interaction_length(water) / cm     # 75.37\nmean_excitation_energy(water) / eV         # 78.0\n\nA material can also be given by its density only, Material{T}(\"iron\"; density=7.874), which is enough for the navigation and the mass thickness of xray; its radiation and interaction lengths are then unknown (NaN).","title":"Materials"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Volumes-and-placements","page":"User Guide","text":"A Volume (Geant4's logical volume) is a shape filled with a material. Daughter volumes are placed in it with place_daughter! (Geant4's physical volume placement), which records a PlacedVolume with the transformation. The same volume can be placed many times, in the same or in different mothers. The daughters must be inside the mother and must not overlap each other; this is not checked.\n\nworld  = Volume{T}(\"world\", Box{T}(1000, 1000, 1000), Material{T}(\"air\"; density=0.0012))\nlayer = Volume{T}(\"module\", Box{T}(100, 100, 10), Material{T}(\"iron\"; density=7.87))\nfor i in -2:2\n    place_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 100i), layer)\nend\nworld[3]          # the volume of the 3rd daughter\n\nAn Assembly groups volumes without a mother shape: placing an assembly places each of its volumes. world_volume returns a world volume that contains a given volume.","title":"Volumes and placements"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#GDML","page":"User Guide","text":"read_gdml(file, T) reads a GDML file, the geometry exchange format of Geant4 and ROOT, and returns its world volume:\n\nworld = read_gdml(\"cms2018.gdml\", Float32)\n\nThe materials, the shapes in the table of supported shapes, the booleans, the volumes, the physical volumes and the assemblies are read, with the units of the file converted to mm and radians. Solids that are not supported are replaced by NoShape with a warning.\n\nThe materials are read as by Geant4: isotopes, elements (by Z and molar mass, or by isotopes), materials of a single element, compounds (<composite>), mixtures of elements and materials (<fraction>), with their density, state, temperature, pressure and mean excitation energy (<MEE>), in the units of the file. Elements and materials that are referenced but not defined in the file are taken from the NIST data (e.g. G4_AIR or Fe).","title":"GDML"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Geometry-model-and-navigation","page":"User Guide","text":"","title":"Geometry model and navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Closing-the-geometry","page":"User Guide","text":"The hierarchy of volumes is convenient to build a geometry, but not to navigate it: its objects have different types, are referenced by pointers and cannot be moved to a GPU. close_geometry converts it to a GeometryModel:\n\nall the data is in arrays of plain (isbits) structures, referenced by indices: the model can be moved to a GPU with Adapt.adapt;\nthe shapes are stored by type, and a shape function is called with shape_call through a generated if chain over the types instead of dynamic dispatch;\nboolean shapes, of any nesting, become flat CSG programs over primitive shapes, and long chains of unions become multi-unions;\nthe daughters of volumes with many daughters are organized in a bounding volume hierarchy (BVH).\n\nmodel = close_geometry(world)\nmodel, index = close_geometry(world; with_index=true)   # index.volumes[i] is the Volume of volume i","title":"Closing the geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Navigating","page":"User Guide","text":"A NavState is the position of a point in the hierarchy: the path of placements from the world to the current volume, and the transformation from the global frame to the frame of the current volume. It is immutable, so it can be kept in registers on a GPU.\n\nlocate(model, point) gives the state of a global point (the deepest volume that contains it).\ncompute_step(model, state, point, dir, step_limit) gives the distance to the next boundary along dir (or step_limit) and the state after crossing it.\nin_world(state) is false once the point has left the world, and material_index(model, state) gives the material of the current volume.\n\nfunction track_length(model, point, dir)\n    state = locate(model, point)\n    length = zero(eltype(point))\n    while in_world(state)\n        step, state = compute_step(model, state, point, dir, typemax(eltype(point)))\n        point += step * dir\n        length += step\n    end\n    return length\nend\n\nAfter crossing a boundary, the new state is computed for the point pushed by push_tolerance beyond it. Like Geant4, the navigator remembers the daughter that a step has just left (\"blocked\" volume), to avoid re-entering it immediately because of rounding errors.","title":"Navigating"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#GPUs","page":"User Guide","text":"The navigator functions are plain Julia functions on isbits data, so they can be called from GPU kernels. KernelAbstractions.jl gives kernels that run on any GPU. xray is an example: it computes an X-ray image of a geometry (the mass thickness along parallel rays, or the material budget in radiation or interaction lengths with quantity=:radlen or :nuclen) on the CPU (with Julia threads) or on a GPU:\n\nworld = read_gdml(\"cms2018.gdml\", Float32)\nmodel = close_geometry(world)\nu, v, image, nsteps = xray(model, 3, 1000)                                  # CPU\nusing Metal\nu, v, image, nsteps = xray(model, 3, 1000; backend=MetalBackend())          # Apple GPU\n\nSome GPU compilers (Apple's in particular) inline all the function calls of a kernel: the size of a kernel grows with the number of shape types of the model and with the number of call sites of the shape functions. This is why the model dispatches without recursion and the CSG programs are flat.","title":"GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Visualization","page":"User Guide","text":"The drawing functions need a Makie backend (GLMakie for interactive windows, CairoMakie for images). draw creates a figure with a shape or a volume hierarchy, and draw! adds it to an existing LScene:\n\nusing GLMakie: GLMakie, Figure, LScene\ndraw(Torus{Float64}(1, 2, 10, 0, 3π/2))\nfig = Figure(); scene = LScene(fig[1, 1])\ndraw!(scene, world; wireframe=true, maxlevel=3)\n\nMakie exports some names that are also Geom4hep shapes (Box, Sphere, ...): import only the Makie names you need, as above, or qualify the shapes (Geom4hep.Box).\n\nmesh(shape) returns a GeometryBasics.Mesh of the surface of a shape, for other visualization or export tools. shape_to_mesh(shape) returns a mesh of triangles oriented outwards with normals at the vertices, smooth on the curved surfaces and sharp at the edges, which is the one that draw uses.","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Benchmarks","page":"User Guide","text":"benchmark_shape times the shape functions of a shape with the methodology of the VecGeom shape benchmarks: the shape in a world box, points sampled in the box (half of them inside the shape) and rays of which 80% hit the shape. vecgeom_suite has the setups of the VecGeom benchmark programs for the shapes of Geom4hep. The script examples/Benchmarks.jl runs the suite and, given a VecGeom build, runs the VecGeom programs with the same setups and compares the times:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/Benchmarks.jl --vecgeom <vecgeom-build>/test/benchmark Polycone","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#Benchmarks","page":"Benchmarks","text":"This example times the shape functions with the methodology of the shape benchmarks of VecGeom, so that the results can be compared with it.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#Table-of-contents","page":"Benchmarks","text":"Pages = [\"Benchmarks.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#Loading-the-modules","page":"Benchmarks","text":"using Geom4hep, Printf\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, Axis, barplot!","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#Benchmarking-a-shape","page":"Benchmarks","text":"benchmark_shape places the shape in a world box, samples points in the box (half of them inside the shape, as VecGeom) and directions (with 80% of the rays from outside hitting the shape), and times each shape function. :shape calls the function on the shape directly, :model through the dispatch of a GeometryModel, as the navigator does. The result has one row per mode and function, with the time per call in ns.\n\nrows = benchmark_shape(Torus{Float64}(1.2, 3.1, 5, 0, 2π); npoints=1024, nrep=10, verbose=false)\nfor r in rows\n    @printf \"%-6s %-16s %8.1f ns\\n\" r.mode r.op r.ns\nend\n\nshape  inside               12.1 ns\nshape  contains             12.2 ns\nshape  distance_to_in      812.9 ns\nshape  safety_to_in         11.1 ns\nshape  distance_to_out     465.0 ns\nshape  safety_to_out         8.9 ns\nmodel  inside               12.7 ns\nmodel  contains             15.2 ns\nmodel  distance_to_in      817.2 ns\nmodel  safety_to_in         12.2 ns\nmodel  distance_to_out     471.4 ns\nmodel  safety_to_out        11.4 ns\n","title":"Benchmarking a shape"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#The-VecGeom-suite","page":"Benchmarks","text":"vecgeom_suite has the setups of the VecGeom benchmark programs (test/benchmark/*Benchmark.cpp) for the shapes of Geom4hep: the same shape parameters, world box, placement and numbers of points.\n\nsuite = vecgeom_suite(Float64)\n[(b.name, b.vecgeom) for b in suite][1:6]\n\n6-element Vector{Tuple{String, String}}:\n (\"Box\", \"BoxBenchmark\")\n (\"Trd\", \"TrdBenchmark\")\n (\"Trap type 0\", \"TrapezoidBenchmark -type 0\")\n (\"Trap type 2\", \"TrapezoidBenchmark -type 2\")\n (\"Trap type 3\", \"TrapezoidBenchmark -type 3\")\n (\"Trap type 4\", \"TrapezoidBenchmark -type 4\")\n\nRunning the suite gives the time per call of each function for each shape:\n\nresults = Dict(b.name => benchmark_shape(b; verbose=false) for b in suite)\nops = (:inside, :distance_to_in, :distance_to_out, :safety_to_in, :safety_to_out)\n@printf \"%-18s\" \"case\"; foreach(op -> @printf(\"%16s\", op), ops); println()\nfor b in suite\n    @printf \"%-18s\" b.name\n    for op in ops\n        @printf \"%16.1f\" only(r.ns for r in results[b.name] if r.mode == :shape && r.op == op)\n    end\n    println()\nend\n\ncase                        inside  distance_to_in distance_to_out    safety_to_in   safety_to_out\nBox                            5.1            30.9             6.4            11.4             3.7\nTrd                           17.2            53.5            26.8            14.6             4.5\nTrap type 0                   26.3            49.5            31.1            18.9            13.4\nTrap type 2                   25.9            53.2            36.2            18.9            13.0\nTrap type 3                   26.2            50.0            37.1            19.1            13.0\nTrap type 4                   26.1            50.6            37.5            18.9            13.4\nTrap type 5                   26.2            50.8            35.9            19.2            13.0\nTube                           9.1            33.9            12.0             5.6             4.4\nCone                          13.4            57.8            55.8            12.9             7.3\nCutTube                       25.3           151.4            93.4            28.4            20.0\nSphere                        10.3            58.5            19.4             9.3             6.2\nOrb                           11.1            61.3            30.2             9.3             6.2\nPolycone type 0               16.7            75.3            80.0            33.9            48.8\nPolycone type 1               13.0            51.0            41.6            15.2            21.5\nPolycone type 2               16.0            61.2            72.0            32.5            48.0\nPolycone type 3               16.4            84.6            83.7            34.3            54.8\nPolyhedron type 0             66.7           249.1           316.5           121.0           140.3\nPolyhedron type 1             67.4           334.7           318.2           103.0           144.1\nPolyhedron type 2             62.7           415.3           248.0            91.8           140.3\nPolyhedron type 3             68.1           347.1           320.5           105.8           149.3\nPolyhedron type 4             93.5           778.0           512.5           168.5           164.9\nPolyhedron type 5             59.4           240.2           330.7           117.0           147.5\nTorus                         12.3           746.6           464.5            11.0             9.0\nMultiUnion                   244.4          1017.3           808.1           707.1           873.6\n\n\nThe times per call of distance_to_in, for each shape:\n\nnames = [b.name for b in suite]\ntimes = [only(r.ns for r in results[n] if r.mode == :shape && r.op == :distance_to_in) for n in names]\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel=\"ns per call\", yscale=log10, xticks=(1:length(names), names),\n          xticklabelrotation=π/3, title=\"distance_to_in\")\nbarplot!(ax, 1:length(names), times)\nfig\n\n<img width=900 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"The VecGeom suite"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Benchmarks/#Comparison-with-VecGeom","page":"Benchmarks","text":"The script examples/Benchmarks.jl of the repository runs the suite and, given a VecGeom build configured with -DBUILD_TESTING=ON -DVECGEOM_TEST_BENCHMARK=ON, the VecGeom benchmark programs with the same numbers of points and repetitions, and prints the ratios of the times:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/Benchmarks.jl --vecgeom <vecgeom-build>/test/benchmark\n\nNote that the timings of these examples, run while building the documentation on a shared machine, are only indicative.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Comparison with VecGeom"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Running-the-examples","page":"Running the examples","text":"The pages in this section are generated from the Literate sources in docs/src/examples and run with the current version of Geom4hep.jl when the documentation is built. They show the code together with its output and plots. Each page has links to download the example as a Jupyter notebook or a plain Julia script.\n\nExample Content\nShapes Creating shapes, calling the shape functions and drawing them\nVolumes Building a hierarchy of volumes and drawing it\nNavigation Closing a geometry, following rays through it and X-ray images\nGDML Reading GDML files: the CMS 2018 detector\nRoundCube Porting a Geant4 solid built from boolean operations, and checking it\nBenchmarks Timing the shape functions with the methodology of VecGeom","title":"Running the examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Setting-up-an-environment","page":"Running the examples","text":"The examples use extra packages for plotting (CairoMakie or GLMakie). The simplest way to run them is with the environment of this documentation, which lists every package they need:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/JuliaHEP/Geom4hep.jl.git\ncd Geom4hep.jl\njulia --project=docs -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/src/examples/Shapes.jl\n\nTo run an example downloaded from these pages instead, put it in a directory with the Project.toml of the documentation, run julia --project=. -e 'import Pkg; Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/JuliaHEP/Geom4hep.jl\"); Pkg.instantiate()', and then the example, or open the notebook in Jupyter.\n\nThe repository has more examples in the examples directory, with their own environment: X-ray images on GPUs (XRay.jl), density slices of the CMS detector (Render.jl), interactive drawing of a detector (DrawDetector.jl) and the comparison of the shape benchmarks with VecGeom (Benchmarks.jl).","title":"Setting up an environment"},{"category":"section","location":"#Geom4hep.jl","page":"Introduction","text":"A native Julia geometry modeller for high energy physics, in the spirit of the Geant4 and VecGeom geometry: shapes (solids), boolean combinations, hierarchical volumes and materials, and a navigator that runs on CPUs and GPUs.\n\nGeom4hep.jl gives you:\n\nShapes with the same parameters as the Geant4 solids (Box, Tube, Cone, Trap, Sphere, Torus, Polycone, Polyhedra, ...), boolean shapes and multi-unions, and the functions that a particle transport needs: inside, distance_to_in, distance_to_out, safety_to_in, safety_to_out and normal.\nGeometry models: a hierarchy of logical volumes (Volume) placed inside each other, built in Julia or read from a GDML file with read_gdml.\nNavigation on CPUs and GPUs: close_geometry turns a hierarchy of volumes into a flat GeometryModel of plain arrays, without dynamic dispatch, that a navigator (locate, compute_step) traverses with the same code on the CPU and in GPU kernels written with KernelAbstractions.jl (Metal, CUDA, AMDGPU).\nVisualization of shapes and volumes with Makie.\nBenchmarks of the shape functions with the methodology of VecGeom, to compare with it.\n\nAll the types are parametric on the floating point type, Float64 or Float32 (most GPUs, and Apple GPUs in particular, are much faster, or only work, in single precision).\n\nThe geometry concepts are those of Geant4: see the chapter Geometry of the Geant4 Application Developer Guide for a description of the solids and of logical and physical volumes.","title":"Geom4hep.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Introduction","text":"Geom4hep.jl is not yet registered. Install it from GitHub:\n\njulia> using Pkg\njulia> Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/JuliaHEP/Geom4hep.jl\")\n\nThe drawing functions are a package extension, activated when a Makie backend (GLMakie, CairoMakie or WGLMakie) is loaded. To run on a GPU, load the KernelAbstractions backend of your GPU: Metal, CUDA or AMDGPU.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Introduction","text":"This example builds a box of water with a lead ball inside, closes the geometry and follows a ray through it. The User Guide explains each part.\n\nusing Geom4hep\nconst T = Float64\n\n# Volumes: shape and material\nworld = Volume{T}(\"world\", Box{T}(100, 100, 100), Material{T}(\"vacuum\"; density=0))\nwater = Volume{T}(\"water\", Box{T}(50, 50, 50), Material{T}(\"water\"; density=1.0))\nball  = Volume{T}(\"ball\", Sphere{T}(0, 20), Material{T}(\"lead\"; density=11.35))\n\n# Placements: the ball at (10, 0, 0) of the water box, the water box at the centre of the world\nplace_daughter!(water, Transformation3D{T}(10, 0, 0), ball)\nplace_daughter!(world, one(Transformation3D{T}), water)\n\n# Flat model for navigation\nmodel = close_geometry(world)\n\n# Follow a ray from `point` along `dir` until it leaves the world\nfunction trace(model, point, dir)\n    state = locate(model, point)\n    while in_world(state)\n        step, next = compute_step(model, state, point, dir, T(Inf))\n        println(\"step of \", step, \" mm in material \", material_index(model, state))\n        point += step * dir\n        state = next\n    end\nend\ntrace(model, Point3{T}(-90, 0, 0), Vector3{T}(1, 0, 0))","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Status","page":"Introduction","text":"The package is being revived and re-organized. The shapes most used in detector descriptions are supported (see the table of supported shapes); the full CMS 2018 GDML geometry can be navigated, on CPUs and on Apple GPUs. Next steps are more solids (Para, ExtrudedSolid, TessellatedSolid, ...), material properties and safety distances for boolean shapes. See the Release Notes.","title":"Status"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#RoundCube","page":"RoundCube","text":"This example builds a cube with rounded edges and corners out of boolean shapes. It is a port to Geom4hep of the custom solid RoundCube defined in C++ in the UserLib example of Geant4.jl (UserLibrary.cpp), where it is a G4VSolid that delegates all its methods to a tree of G4UnionSolid and G4SubtractionSolid. It shows how to translate the boolean solids and the transformations of Geant4 to Geom4hep.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"RoundCube"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Table-of-contents","page":"RoundCube","text":"Pages = [\"RoundCube.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Loading-the-modules","page":"RoundCube","text":"RotZXZ (rotations given by Euler angles) is not exported by Geom4hep, but it is available from the Rotations package that Geom4hep uses.\n\nusing Geom4hep, Geom4hep.Units\nusing Geom4hep.Rotations: RotZXZ\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, Axis, LScene, heatmap!, Colorbar\n\nconst T = Float64;","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#From-Geant4-to-Geom4hep","page":"RoundCube","text":"The Geant4 solids map one to one to the Geom4hep shapes, with the same parameters:\n\nGeant4 Geom4hep\nG4Box(\"cube\", ca, ca, ca) Box{T}(ca, ca, ca)\nG4Tubs(\"cyl\", 0, r, ca, 0, 2 * M_PI) Tube{T}(0, r, ca, 0, 2π)\nG4Orb(\"orb\", r) Sphere{T}(0, r)\nG4SubtractionSolid(\"s\", A, B, t) BooleanSubtraction(A, B, t)\nG4UnionSolid(\"u\", A, B, t) BooleanUnion(A, B, t)\n\nThe transformations need more care. A G4Transform3D(rot, trans) places the second solid in the frame of the first one with the rotation rot applied to the solid, while a Transformation3D holds the rotation of the frame of the mother into the frame of the daughter, which is the inverse. A G4RotationMatrix(φ, θ, ψ) is built from Euler angles as the inverse of the rotation by φ around z, θ around the new x and ψ around the new z. The two inversions cancel, and G4Transform3D(G4RotationMatrix(φ, θ, ψ), G4ThreeVector(x, y, z)) becomes Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z, RotZXZ{T}(φ, θ, ψ)). A small helper makes the translation of the C++ code almost literal:\n\ng4transform(x, y, z, φ=0, θ=0, ψ=0) = Transformation3D{T}(x, y, z, RotZXZ{T}(φ, θ, ψ));","title":"From Geant4 to Geom4hep"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Building-the-RoundCube","page":"RoundCube","text":"The C++ constructor RoundCube(a, r) translated line by line. A quarter s4 of the cube (a cube of side a/4 with three edges and one corner rounded with radius r) is cut out with subtractions. Four copies of it are glued to a central frame cube to make half of the cube, and the half is joined with itself rotated by π around z. The result is a cube of side a (half length a/2).\n\nfunction roundcube(a, r)\n    ca = a / 4\n    cube  = Box{T}(ca, ca, ca)\n    cyl   = Tube{T}(0, r, ca, 0, 2π)\n    orb   = Sphere{T}(0, r)\n\n    edge  = Box{T}(r, r, ca)\n    vert  = Box{T}(r, r, r)\n    frame = Box{T}(ca, ca, ca)\n\n    # the material to remove along an edge (acyl) and at a corner (aorb)\n    acyl1 = BooleanSubtraction(edge, cyl, g4transform(-r, -r, 0))\n    acyl2 = BooleanSubtraction(edge, cyl, g4transform(-r, r, 0))\n    acyl3 = BooleanSubtraction(edge, cyl, g4transform(r, r, 0))\n    aorb  = BooleanSubtraction(vert, orb, g4transform(-r, -r, -r))\n\n    # a corner of the cube with three rounded edges and a rounded vertex\n    s1 = BooleanSubtraction(cube, acyl1, g4transform(ca, ca, 0))\n    s2 = BooleanSubtraction(s1, acyl2, g4transform(ca, 0, ca, 0, π/2, 0))\n    s3 = BooleanSubtraction(s2, acyl3, g4transform(0, ca, ca, 0, π/2, π/2))\n    s4 = BooleanSubtraction(s3, aorb, g4transform(ca, ca, ca))\n\n    # four corners around the frame make half of the cube (x > 0)\n    u1 = BooleanUnion(frame, s4, g4transform(ca, ca, ca))\n    u2 = BooleanUnion(u1, s4, g4transform(ca, ca, -ca, 0, π/2, 0))\n    u3 = BooleanUnion(u2, s4, g4transform(ca, -ca, ca, 0, -π/2, 0))\n    u4 = BooleanUnion(u3, s4, g4transform(ca, -ca, -ca, 0, π/2, π/2))\n\n    # and the other half, rotated by π around z\n    return BooleanUnion(u4, u4, g4transform(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, π))\nend\n\na, r = 10cm, 1cm\nrcube = roundcube(a, r);\n\nThe extent is the cube of side a:\n\nextent(rcube)\n\n([-50.00000000000001, -50.00000000000001, -50.0], [50.0, 50.0, 50.0])","title":"Building the RoundCube"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Checking-the-shape","page":"RoundCube","text":"A rounded cube has a simple signed distance function: negative inside, positive outside. Comparing it with inside at random points checks every transformation of the construction (with the inverse rotations, thousands of points would disagree).\n\nfunction sdf(p, h, r)\n    q = abs.(p) .- (h - r)\n    return norm(max.(q, 0)) - r + min(maximum(q), 0)\nend\npoints = [Point3{T}((rand(3) .- 0.5) .* 1.2a) for _ in 1:100_000]\nmismatches = count(points) do p\n    d = sdf(p, a/2, r)\n    abs(d) > 1e-6 && (inside(rcube, p) == kOutside) != (d > 0)\nend\n\n0\n\nThe volume, estimated by sampling for boolean shapes, can be compared with the exact one: a cube of side a - 2r, six slabs on its faces, quarter cylinders along the 12 edges and eighths of a sphere at the 8 corners.\n\nb = a - 2r\nexact = b^3 + 6b^2 * r + 3π * r^2 * b + 4π/3 * r^3\ncapacity(rcube) / exact\n\n1.0000061359048178","title":"Checking the shape"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Shape-functions","page":"RoundCube","text":"The boolean shape answers all the shape functions. A ray along the diagonal enters the cube at a rounded corner, at a distance √3(a/2 - r) + r from the center:\n\ndir = normalize(Vector3{T}(-1, -1, -1))\np = Point3{T}(10cm, 10cm, 10cm)\ndistance_to_in(rcube, p, dir), norm(p) - (√3 * (a/2 - r) + r)\n\n(93.92304845413263, 93.92304845413264)\n\nThe normal on a rounded edge points away from the axis of the edge:\n\nnormal(rcube, Point3{T}(a/2 - r + r/√2, a/2 - r + r/√2, 1cm))\n\n3-element Geom4hep.Vector3{Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 0.7071067811865476\n 0.7071067811865476\n 0.0\n\nThe operands touch along internal seams (the planes x = 0, y = 0 and z = 0), and overlap elsewhere (the central cube ends at ±a/4, inside the corners). Points on a seam are inside, and the points where a seam meets the surface are on the surface, with the normal of the surface. At the centre of a face, four operands meet:\n\np_seam, p_face = Point3{T}(0, 0, 3cm), Point3{T}(a/2, 0, 0)\ninside(rcube, p_seam) == kInside, inside(rcube, p_face) == kSurface, normal(rcube, p_face)\n\n(true, true, [1.0, 0.0, -0.0])\n\nChecking the classification and the normals at random points of the surface, many of them on the seams or on the planes where the operands overlap:\n\nfunction surface_point(q, h, r)                     # projection on the surface, and the normal\n    c = clamp.(q, -(h - r), h - r)\n    d = q - c\n    norm(d) > 0 && return c + r * normalize(d), normalize(d)\n    i = argmax(abs.(q))\n    n = Vector3{T}(ntuple(j -> j == i ? sign(q[i]) : 0, 3))\n    return Point3{T}(ntuple(j -> j == i ? sign(q[i]) * h : q[j], 3)), n\nend\nseams = (0, a/4, -a/4)\nbad = 0\nfor _ in 1:5000\n    q = (rand(3) .- 0.5) .* 1.2a\n    for _ in 1:rand(0:2)\n        q[rand(1:3)] = rand(seams)                  # move to one or two of these planes\n    end\n    p, n = surface_point(Point3{T}(q...), a/2, r)\n    global bad += inside(rcube, p) != kSurface || normal(rcube, p) ⋅ n < 0.9999\nend\nbad\n\n0","title":"Shape functions"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Drawing","page":"RoundCube","text":"The mesh of a boolean shape is computed with boolean operations on the meshes of its operands (csg_mesh), so draw shows the surface of the result. Here the RoundCube and one of its corners (s4, the right operand of the unions), also as a wireframe that shows the triangles of the mesh:\n\ncorner = rcube.left.right\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 300))\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 1], show_axis=false), rcube)\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 2], show_axis=false), corner)\ndraw!(LScene(fig[1, 3], show_axis=false), corner; wireframe=true)\nfig\n\n<img width=900 height=300 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\nThe mesh is closed, and the volume it encloses is close to the exact one (the curved surfaces are approximated by 64 facets):\n\nm = csg_mesh(rcube)\nP, F = coordinates(m), faces(m)\nsum(f -> P[f[1]] ⋅ (P[f[2]] × P[f[3]]), F) / 6 / exact\n\n0.9994835331531164\n\ndraw_distance_to_in checks the surface independently of the mesh: it shoots random rays and draws the points where they enter the shape:\n\ndraw_distance_to_in(rcube, 50_000)\n\n<img width=600 height=450 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\nA slice of inside through a corner region shows the rounded edges and vertex:\n\nxs = range(0, 0.6a, length=300)\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 400))\nfor (i, z) in enumerate((0, a/2 - r/2))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], aspect=1, title=\"z = $(z) mm\", xlabel=\"x (mm)\", ylabel=\"y (mm)\")\n    heatmap!(ax, xs, xs, [Int(inside(rcube, Point3{T}(x, y, z))) for x in xs, y in xs], colormap=:grays)\nend\nfig\n\n<img width=900 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Drawing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/RoundCube/#Using-it-in-a-geometry","page":"RoundCube","text":"Like any other shape, the RoundCube can fill volumes and be navigated. Here a few of them of different materials and radii of the edges are placed in a world box, and an X-ray image is made (see the Navigation example).\n\nworld = Volume{T}(\"world\", Box{T}(40cm, 40cm, 15cm), Material{T}(\"air\"; density=0.0012))\nfor (i, (radius, density)) in enumerate(((0.5cm, 2.7), (1.5cm, 7.87), (3cm, 11.35), (4cm, 1.0)))\n    vol = Volume{T}(\"rcube$i\", roundcube(20cm, radius), Material{T}(\"m$i\"; density=density))\n    x, y = (i - 1) % 2 == 0 ? -18cm : 18cm, (i - 1) ÷ 2 == 0 ? -18cm : 18cm\n    place_daughter!(world, Transformation3D{T}(x, y, 0, 0, 0, π/8), vol)\nend\nmodel = close_geometry(world)\nu, v, image, _ = xray(model, 3, 400)\nfig = Figure(size=(600, 500))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], aspect=1, xlabel=\"x (mm)\", ylabel=\"y (mm)\")\nhm = heatmap!(ax, u, v, image, colormap=:grays)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm, label=\"mass thickness (g/cm³ × mm)\")\nfig\n\n<img width=600 height=500 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Using it in a geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"This example creates the shapes of Geom4hep, calls the shape functions on them and draws them. The shapes have the parameters of the corresponding Geant4 solids.\n\nnote: Note that\nYou can also download this example as a Jupyter notebook and a plain Julia source file.","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Table-of-contents","page":"Shapes","text":"Pages = [\"Shapes.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Table of contents"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Loading-the-modules","page":"Shapes","text":"CairoMakie draws into images; use GLMakie instead for interactive windows. Makie exports names that are also shapes of Geom4hep (e.g. Box), so only the names that are needed are imported from it.\n\nusing Geom4hep, Geom4hep.Units\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie: CairoMakie, Figure, LScene\n\nconst T = Float64;","title":"Loading the modules"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Creating-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"The constructors take the floating point type as parameter, and the parameters of the shape with the conventions of the Geant4 solids: half lengths, radii, and angular ranges given by a start angle and an extent. Lengths are in mm and angles in radians; the units of Geom4hep.Units make this explicit.\n\nbox    = Box{T}(10cm, 15cm, 20cm)\ntube   = Tube{T}(5cm, 10cm, 20cm, 0, 270deg)\ncone   = Cone{T}(2cm, 5cm, 5cm, 10cm, 20cm, 0, 2π)\ntrd    = Trd{T}(10cm, 5cm, 15cm, 8cm, 20cm)\ntrap   = Trap{T}(20cm, 10deg, 30deg, 10cm, 8cm, 12cm, 5deg, 6cm, 5cm, 7cm, 5deg)\nsphere = Sphere{T}(8cm, 10cm, 0, 270deg, 30deg, 120deg)\ntorus  = Torus{T}(2cm, 4cm, 12cm, 0, 270deg)\npcone  = Polycone{T}([0, 5cm, 5cm, 0], [8cm, 10cm, 10cm, 6cm], [-20cm, -10cm, 10cm, 20cm], 0, 2π)\nphedra = Polyhedra{T}(6, [3cm, 3cm, 3cm], [6cm, 10cm, 6cm], [-20cm, 0, 20cm], 0, 2π)\nctube  = CutTube{T}(5cm, 10cm, 20cm, 0, 2π, Vector3{T}(0, -0.5, -1), Vector3{T}(0.5, 0, 1));\n\nPrinting a shape shows its parameters\n\ntorus\n\nTorus{Float64}(rmin = 20.0, rmax = 40.0, rtor = 120.0, ϕ₀ = 0.0, Δϕ = 4.71238898038469)","title":"Creating shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Drawing-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"With a Makie backend loaded, draw(shape) creates a figure with the shape, and draw! adds it to a scene. Here all the shapes are drawn in a grid.\n\nshapes = [box, tube, cone, trd, trap, sphere, torus, pcone, phedra, ctube]\nfig = Figure(size=(1000, 450))\nfor (i, s) in enumerate(shapes)\n    scene = LScene(fig[(i-1) ÷ 5 + 1, (i-1) % 5 + 1], show_axis=false)\n    draw!(scene, s)\nend\nfig\n\n<img width=1000 height=450 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Drawing shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Shape-functions","page":"Shapes","text":"The shape functions take points and directions in the frame of the shape. inside classifies a point as kInside, kSurface or kOutside:\n\np_in, p_out = Point3{T}(0, 0, 0), Point3{T}(30cm, 0, 0)\ninside(box, p_in) == kInside, inside(box, p_out) == kOutside, inside(box, Point3{T}(10cm, 0, 0)) == kSurface\n\n(true, true, true)\n\ndistance_to_in is the distance along a direction from an outside point to the shape, and distance_to_out from an inside point to the surface:\n\ndir = Vector3{T}(-1, 0, 0)\ndistance_to_in(box, p_out, dir), distance_to_out(box, p_in, dir)\n\n(200.0, 100.0)\n\nA ray that misses the shape gets Inf:\n\ndistance_to_in(box, p_out, Vector3{T}(0, 1, 0))\n\nInf\n\nThe safeties are lower bounds of the distance to the surface in any direction:\n\nsafety_to_in(box, p_out), safety_to_out(box, Point3{T}(5cm, 0, 0))\n\n(200.0, 50.0)\n\nnormal gives the outward normal at a point of the surface, extent the corners of a bounding box, and capacity and surface the volume and the area:\n\nnormal(sphere, Point3{T}(0, 10cm, 0))\n\n3-element Geom4hep.Vector3{Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 0.0\n 1.0\n 0.0\n\nextent(torus)\n\n([-160.0, -160.0, -40.0], [160.0, 160.0, 40.0])\n\ncapacity(box) ≈ 8 * 10cm * 15cm * 20cm, surface(box)\n\n(true, 520000.0)","title":"Shape functions"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Checking-the-distances","page":"Shapes","text":"draw_distance_to_in shoots random rays from outside a shape and draws the points where they enter it; draw_distance_to_out does the same from inside. Both draw the surface of the shape, which is a good visual test of the distance functions.\n\ndraw_distance_to_out(phedra, 50_000)\n\n<img width=600 height=450 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Checking the distances"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Boolean-shapes","page":"Shapes","text":"Boolean shapes combine two shapes; the second one is placed in the frame of the first one with a Transformation3D. They can be nested.\n\nhole  = Tube{T}(0, 4cm, 30cm, 0, 2π)\nframe = BooleanSubtraction(box, hole)\nframe = BooleanSubtraction(frame, hole, Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 0, π/2, 0, 0))\nframe = BooleanUnion(frame, Sphere{T}(0, 6cm), Transformation3D{T}(0, 0, 20cm))\ninside(frame, Point3{T}(0, 0, 0)) == kOutside, inside(frame, Point3{T}(0, 0, 24cm)) == kInside\n\n(true, true)\n\nTheir mesh is computed with boolean operations on the meshes of the operands (csg_mesh), so they are drawn like any other shape:\n\ndraw(frame)\n\n<img width=600 height=450 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n\nThe rays shot by draw_distance_to_in check the result independently of the mesh:\n\ndraw_distance_to_in(frame, 50_000)\n\n<img width=600 height=450 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>","title":"Boolean shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/Shapes/#Single-precision","page":"Shapes","text":"All the shapes can also be created in single precision, which is what most GPUs need. The surfaces are then thicker (tolerance(Float32) is 1 µm) to absorb the rounding errors.\n\nbox32 = Box{Float32}(10cm, 15cm, 20cm)\ndistance_to_in(box32, Point3{Float32}(30cm, 0, 0), Vector3{Float32}(-1, 0, 0)), tolerance(Float32)\n\n(200.0f0, 0.001f0)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Single precision"}]
}
